import blink.ffi

// br q3ssqw — an UNANNOTATED `let` bound to a CALL returning `Ptr[T]` loses the pointee, so
// the local is declared `void*` and every dereference through it is a C-compiler error with no
// Blink span at all.
//
// THE CAUSE, in one line: `emit_let_binding`'s declaration chain has a `ptr_ann` branch (which
// reads the pointee out of the binding's own type annotation) and a `ptr_ffi` branch (which
// reads it out of `ffi_offset_pending_struct`), and NOTHING for a `Ptr` whose pointee is known
// only to typecheck — so it falls through to the final `else` and spells `c_type_str(CT_PTR)`,
// which is `void*`. The tid already holds the pointee: `c_type_from_tid` answers `uint8_t*` /
// `int64_t*` for every one of these rows, which is how the Stage 3a ctypediv sweep found them.
//
// THE AXIS IS THE MISSING ANNOTATION, not `as_cstr` / `take` / `deref`. The annotated form of
// each row below already worked and is pinned at the bottom, which is what makes the branch and
// not the pointer machinery the defect.
//
// THE FAILURE MODE IS A CC ESCAPE — the worst diagnostic class, because there is no Blink line
// to point at:
//
//     fn main() {
//         let s = "hi"
//         let p = s.as_cstr()
//         let v = p.deref()
//         let n: Int = v
//         io.println("n={n}")
//     }
//
//     build/as_cstr.c:123:23: error: void value not ignored as it ought to be
//       123 |     const int64_t v = (*(p));
//
// Note the SECOND erasure on that same emitted line: `const int64_t v`, where the Blink type of
// `p.deref()` is `U8`. The deref's own result type is derived from the receiver's flat pointee
// too, so both halves of the line come from the same missing channel — which is why these rows
// assert the VALUE and not merely that the program compiles.
//
// WHY THE CORPUS NEVER CAUGHT IT. Six corpus files carry these seven rows (tests/test_ffi.bl
// ×2, test_ptr_string.bl, test_libc_bytes.bl, test_yb9ytb_read_fully.bl, test_ffi_scope_with.bl,
// test_ps5br9_ffi_scope_receiver_type.bl) and every one of them calls only `.is_null()` or
// `.to_str()` on the erased local — neither dereferences. A zero-failure corpus at a live
// divergence is an unexercised tap, not coverage (feedback_corpus_sweep_is_not_coverage); these
// rows are the tap being exercised.
//
// UPDATE (br 0dtbe6, br axpq22). `deref()` on a `Ptr[U8]` is a `U8`: the result carries the
// POINTEE's own type, and typecheck now SIGNS it, so a bare `let n: Int = <Ptr[U8]>.deref()`
// is a TypeError (E0300) exactly like the explicit `let u: U8 = 200; let n: Int = u` store —
// no silent U8-into-Int launder. The rows below therefore read the pointee back through the
// honest `.deref().to_int()`, which still exercises this test's axis (the pointee survives an
// unannotated binding) without depending on the closed fail-open.
//
// NOT this ticket, checked rather than assumed: `Ptr.offset()` through an erased pointee does
// NOT silently miscompute a stride. It is gated by `error[FfiOffsetUnknownStride]` ("requires a
// Ptr to an @ffi.struct typedef so the stride is known"), which fires on a scalar pointee
// whether or not the binding is annotated — so a byte-walk row (`p.offset(1).deref()`) cannot be
// written here at all, and there is no silent wrong-address shape to pin. That path fails closed,
// which is the right shape for a stride the compiler genuinely does not know.

// ── The cc-escape rows ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// The smallest shape in the language that reaches this: no ffi import, no scope, one builtin
// method. `Str.as_cstr()` is also the exact producer in tests/test_ptr_string.bl, so this is a
// corpus shape with one line added, not a synthetic one.
test "a Str.as_cstr() result derefs through an unannotated binding" {
    let s = "hi"
    let p = s.as_cstr()
    let v = p.deref()
    // `deref()` on a Ptr[U8] is a U8, and the pointee survives the unannotated binding;
    // the widening to Int is the explicit `.to_int()` (a bare store is E0300, br axpq22).
    let n: Int = v.to_int()
    assert_eq(n, 104)
}

// A scope-allocated Int cell handed back out of the scope by `take`, which is the shape the
// corpus rows in tests/test_ffi.bl carry.
test "a scope.take() result derefs through an unannotated binding" {
    with ffi.scope() as scope {
        let p: Ptr[Int] = scope.alloc()
        p.write(7)
        let taken = scope.take(p)
        assert_eq(taken.deref(), 7)
    }
}

// `scope.cstr()` is the other Ptr[U8] producer, and it goes through the scope rather than
// through a Str method — a different call path to the same erased binding.
test "a scope.cstr() result derefs through an unannotated binding" {
    with ffi.scope() as scope {
        let s = scope.cstr("hi")
        let n: Int = s.deref().to_int()
        assert_eq(n, 104)
    }
}

// NO user-fn row, and that is a language fact rather than a gap in this test: an ordinary Blink
// fn cannot return `Ptr[T]` at all. `error[PtrOutsideFFI]` (typecheck.bl:4253) restricts the type
// to `@ffi` functions and `@trusted` blocks, `@trusted(audit:ID)` on the fn does not satisfy it
// (it wants `@ffi` or a trusted BLOCK), and `@ffi` in turn demands declared effects and a
// foreign body. So every producer of a `Ptr[T]` value is a builtin, an intrinsic, or a scope
// method — which is exactly what the rows above cover, by three different call paths.

// Two hops, because the pointee must survive being re-bound as well as being produced. The
// intermediate `let` is itself an erased binding under the defect, so this row fails even if
// the producing call is fixed but the rebind is not.
test "the pointee survives a rebind of an unannotated binding" {
    let s = "hi"
    let p = s.as_cstr()
    let q = p
    let n: Int = q.deref().to_int()
    assert_eq(n, 104)
}

// ── Regression pins ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// The annotated control. This is the row that proves the axis: identical code, one annotation,
// and it worked before the fix — so the fix must not change it.
test "an ANNOTATED binding was never affected" {
    with ffi.scope() as scope {
        let q: Ptr[Int] = scope.alloc()
        q.write(9)
        let t2: Ptr[Int] = scope.take(q)
        assert_eq(t2.deref(), 9)
    }
}

test "an ANNOTATED Ptr[U8] was never affected" {
    let s = "hi"
    let p: Ptr[U8] = s.as_cstr()
    let n: Int = p.deref().to_int()
    assert_eq(n, 104)
}

// The two methods every corpus row actually calls. They worked on a `void*`-declared local
// (neither dereferences), so they are the pins that a fix must not disturb.
test "is_null and to_str on an unannotated binding still work" {
    let s = "hello world"
    let p = s.as_cstr()
    assert_eq(p.is_null(), false)
    match p.to_str() {
        Some(val) => assert_eq(val, "hello world")
        None => assert(false)
    }
}

test "addr on an unannotated binding still works" {
    let s = "hi"
    let p = s.as_cstr()
    assert(p.addr() != 0)
}
